// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4)/(5): a
// `consteval` non-static member function is an immediate function, and one
// immediate member function may call another on its own `this`. Inside an
// immediate function the call is *not* an immediate invocation at all
// ([expr.const.imm]/1, adopted unchanged via §9.1(1)/(2) and front-matter
// §1(2)): it is an ordinary subexpression of the enclosing immediate
// evaluation. Only the outermost call from `main` has to satisfy §9.1(4).
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(6.2) requires the
// implementation to support both calls.
//
// Call position: inside another `consteval` function. Callee: `consteval`
// non-static member function. Receiver: `this`.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp resolves the member call but
// then reports "error: internal error: no generated code for resolved
// function 'Counter_report' called here" -- the immediate member call is
// lowered as a runtime call instead of being folded. The expectation above
// comes from the spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the
// implementation.
class Counter {
public:
    virtual ~Counter() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    consteval Counter(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int half() const {
        return this->value / 2;
    }

    consteval int report() const {
        return this->half() + 1;
    }
};

int main() {
    return Counter{82}.report();
}
